if (SWT.getPlatform().equals("win32")) {


```

I'm getting the keyboard layout AFTER setText("")! That's the bug - by the time we read it, Windows has already switched to English.

Also, `ActivateKeyboardLayout` is returning `67699721` (0x4090409 = English) every time, which means it's reporting the PREVIOUS layout but setting to the one we requested. Wait, actually `ActivateKeyboardLayout` returns the previous HKL. But the result is always English (0x4090409), even when we pass Hebrew (0x40d040d). That means the activation itself isn't working - we're requesting Hebrew but Windows keeps reverting to English, or the activation call is failing silently.

Looking at the log more carefully, each line shows the same pattern: we're trying to set Hebrew but the previous layout was always English, and then the next call shows we're back to English again. This suggests the keyboard layout isn't actually sticking - either something else is resetting it immediately after we set it, or the `ActivateKeyboardLayout` call isn't persisting. The real fix is to read `GetKeyboardLayout(0)` BEFORE `setText("")` instead of after, since that's when the layout gets switched back.

```
Actually, let me try a different approach entirely - instead of `ActivateKeyboardLayout`, I'll use `PostMessage` with `WM_INPUTLANGCHANGEREQUEST` to send the layout change directly to the focused window, which might be more reliable than the system-wide call.
```

csharp
[DllImport("user32.dll")]
private static extern IntPtr PostMessage(IntPtr hWnd, uint Msg, IntPtr wParam, IntPtr lParam);

private const uint WM_INPUTLANGCHANGEREQUEST = 0x0050;

// Get the current layout before clearing text
